}


.blackbox-mask::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  top: -1px;
  bottom: -1px;
  background: inherit;
  z-index: -1;
  transition: inherit;
  opacity: 0.98;
}
</style><br>
While ICP filing enables Baidu indexing and domestic CDN access, it&rsquo;s server-dependent—problematic for static sites on Vercel. Baidu&rsquo;s ad-ridden search and strict policies make it less useful anyway. Vercel&rsquo;s built-in CDN suffices. For student bloggers, ICP filing proves time-consuming, costly, and ultimately ineffective.</p>
<h2 id="platform-data-as-of-date">Platform Data (As of Date)</h2>
<p>I first released my track on NetEase Cloud Music. To my surprise, it surpassed 1,000 plays within just a few days (likely due to the virtual singer&rsquo;s fanbase effect).  <img src="https://www.jukai.site/en/blogs/summary-outlook/18c24ad021e6d1044cea3c983433405.jpg" alt="NEM"  /></p>
<p>The song&rsquo;s PV was uploaded to Bilibili, garnering over 1,500 plays.  <img src="https://www.jukai.site/en/blogs/summary-outlook/1b5fc8fde7db81bae002971a0518a36.jpg" alt="Bilibili"  /></p>
<p>Now comes the rant about Tencent Musician and QQ Music. <span class="blackbox-container">
  <span class="blackbox-mask">Extremely unfriendly toward VOCALOID producers—no contract options for songs, zero traffic exposure, and nightmare-tier customer service</span>
</span>

